<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Second Sex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Le Deuxième Sexe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Sex is a 1949 book by the French existentialist philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir">Simone de Beauvoir</a> on the treatment of women in her own society and throughout history, written in about 14 months between 1946 and 1949 and published in two volumes, Facts and Myths and Lived Experience. Beauvoir argued that man is treated as the default and woman as the Other. The first French edition sold around 22,000 copies in a week, and the Vatican placed it on its List of Prohibited Books. Translated into 40 languages, it is regarded as a groundbreaking work of feminist philosophy and a starting point of second-wave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-second-sex.jpg" alt="The Second Sex" width="300" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_deuxième_sexe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Volume One, Facts and Myths, asks what a woman is. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir">Beauvoir</a> compares the physiology of men and women and concludes that values cannot be based on physiology, so that the facts of biology must be read in their ontological, economic and social context. She rejects the views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>, Alfred Adler and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>, and traces the history of women's condition from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, courtly love, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> to trade unions, birth control and women's suffrage. She argues that women's historical insignificance, not an inferiority, has doomed them to inferiority, and she examines the myths about women in writers such as Henry de Montherlant, D. H. Lawrence, Paul Claudel, André Breton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stendhal">Stendhal</a>.</p><p>Volume Two, Lived Experience, follows a woman's life. A girl is taught to be a woman, her feminine destiny is imposed on her by society, and she has no innate maternal instinct. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir">Beauvoir</a> then describes puberty, sexual relations, marriage, motherhood, the work of married women, prostitution and old age. She calls marriage an institution that almost always destroys woman and argues that abortion is not an issue of morality. She concludes that women must work for their own liberation and looks to a future of equality in which men and women affirm their brotherhood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book was condemned by the Vatican and remained on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/index-librorum-prohibitorum">Index Librorum Prohibitorum</a> until the Index itself was abolished in 1966. A Spanish translation printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> was banned in Francoist Spain in 1955, and Spanish feminists smuggled in copies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir">Beauvoir</a> recalled in a 1974 interview that Camus was furious and that professors threw the book across the room, partly because she had spoken about female sexuality. The sex researcher Alfred Kinsey thought it interesting as literature but of no value to science.</p><p>Its attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psychoanalysis">psychoanalysis</a> helped inspire later feminist arguments, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betty-friedan">Betty Friedan</a>'s The Feminine Mystique, Kate Millett's Sexual Politics and Germaine Greer's The Female Eunuch. Camille <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paglia">Paglia</a> calls it the supreme work of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a>. Judith Butler reads the line One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman as separating sex from gender, a reading contested by Toril Moi and Nancy Bauer. Critics have also charged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir">Beauvoir</a> with an unconscious misogyny and with writing from a middle-class standpoint.</p><p>The 1953 English translation by H. M. Parshley, which cut great swaths of the text and mistranslated its philosophical vocabulary, was widely judged poor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir">Beauvoir</a> asked for a more faithful one in a 1985 interview, and a complete translation by Constance Borde and Sheila Malovany-Chevallier appeared in November 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Second Sex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
